Original commit body from 181ab63cf8ebe935ad0b24c05b35cb859de1b554:

Strategy 1.9 already INFERRED the receiver type of `File::create(p)?.write_all(..)`
and dropped it when the in-repo `Type::method` lookup missed — which is exactly
what an external type does. The inference had run; only the carry was missing.

`?` and `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` are the SAME mechanism — a wrapper projecting
`Result<T, E>` to `T` — and are handled together. #595 left `?` alone on purpose
so its shape-3 walker would mirror `_infer_type_from_rust_rhs` exactly; the
unwrapping belongs in the shared helper where both get it, which is what this
item said. The measurement decided the scope: over ripgrep + bellman +
tiktoken-rs a receiver reached through `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` occurs 49 times
against 29 for `?`, so shipping the syntax form alone would have fixed the rarer
half of one mechanism.

`_infer_type_from_rust_rhs` normalises a scoped path to a BARE name, so
`std::fs::File::create(p)?` came back as `File` and the written path was gone —
the package-stripped hazard this item's own ADR-0051 annotation names. It is
recovered from the scoped callee the way #595's `_var_type_paths` does for
annotations, so the slot names `std::fs::File`.

TWO GUARDS, BOTH FOUND BY READING THE MOVED ROWS BACK, NOT BY REASONING:

  (a) The CALL's own callee must not be a projector. In
      `File::create(p).unwrap()` the receiver of `unwrap` is the
      `Result<File, E>`, not the `File`. Without this guard, 135 of 213 new
      ripgrep edges were `unwrap` attributed to `std::fs::File`.
  (b) The RECEIVER must have been explicitly unwrapped. `File::create(p)`
      evaluates to a Result; only `File::create(p)?` or `.unwrap()` evaluates to
      a `File`, and nothing here knows whether a library associated function is
      fallible. A raw `Type::assoc()` receiver is withheld — withhold, never
      pick-first.

MEASURED, every surviving row read back at source:

  ripgrep      typed module slots 431 -> 442,  11 new edges,  0 genuine losses
  bellman      typed module slots 220 -> 233
  tiktoken-rs  typed module slots  66 ->  67

ripgrep's 11: `file.write_all` ×3, `file.set_len` ×2, `file.lines` ×2,
`file.flush`, `h.as_file()` -> `winapi_util::Handle`, and
`sink.stats().unwrap().clone()` -> `crate::stats::Stats`. The 8 A-only rows are
those same sites under `external::` — re-keys, not losses. io-boundaries are
unchanged (ripgrep 30 edges / 25 chains; bellman and tiktoken-rs 0 / 0, the
controls).

THE ITEM'S 580-SITE ESTIMATE IS NOT REACHABLE, and guard (b) is why. I sized the
associated-fn receiver population at 580 and called it the headline before
building it; most of that is a raw `Type::assoc()` receiver whose Result-ness is
undecidable here. The reachable population is the explicitly-unwrapped forms —
29 `?` sites plus 49 `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` sites on this corpus.

`test_rust_field_receiver_module_slot.py`'s shape-C assertion moves from "still
unclassified" to "classifies", as that test's own failure message instructs. Its
note attributed the shape to WI-lalot "because the type is never written down".
The type IS written down — in the producer's own scoped path — so the shape
closes here, with no `std::fs::File::create` row involved.

SEPARATELY, AN INSTRUMENT DEFECT THAT INVALIDATES EARLIER RUST CHURN NUMBERS:
`pair_churn_lang.py` splits a dst on ':' and reads fields POSITIONALLY, which
assumes the module has no colons. A rust module does, so a site whose module
gains `::` reads as one loss plus one gain. That is why this run first reported
42 ripgrep losses where a file:line read-back finds 0. `slot_count.py` anchors on
the span instead. Treat rust churn from that rig as suspect wherever a module is
qualified.

